INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Mandiant, Kaspersky, WIRED and CSA evidence on 2023 3CX compromise", "source_url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/3cx-software-supply-chain-compromise", "text": "Evidence batch: Mandiant says the initial compromise vector of 3CX's network was malicious software downloaded from the Trading Technologies website, specifically a malware-laced X_TRADER installer distributed via an earlier software supply-chain compromise. A 3CX employee installed the compromised X_TRADER, allowing attackers to move through the 3CX network, harvest credentials, move laterally, and compromise Windows and macOS build environments. On Windows, TAXHAUL and COLDCAT persisted via IKEEXT DLL search-order hijacking with LocalSystem; on macOS, POOLRAT persisted via Launch Daemons. The attacker then produced a trojanized 3CX DesktopApp. Affected 3CX DesktopApp versions included Windows 18.12.407/416 and earlier per Mandiant/CSA, and macOS 18.11.1213, 18.12.402/407/416 per CSA. Mandiant describes malicious code running SUDDENICON, extracting C2 servers from encrypted GitHub icons, then downloading ICONICSTEALER, which steals browser information. Mandiant tracks the activity as UNC4736, a suspected North Korean nexus cluster, and assesses with moderate confidence that it is related to financially motivated North Korean AppleJeus activity. Mandiant notes POOLRAT in 3CX linked to an older POOLRAT sample reported by CISA in the AppleJeus CoinGoTrade operation. Kaspersky says the infected MSI contained a malicious DLL, used d3dcompiler_47.dll overlay shellcode and GitHub-hosted icons to obtain C2, then downloaded an infostealer; it also observed Gopuram on fewer than ten machines, especially cryptocurrency companies. Kaspersky attributes the campaign to Lazarus with medium-to-high confidence based on Gopuram coexisting with AppleJeus, shared C2 infrastructure, loader-shellcode similarities, and domains previously attributed to Lazarus. WIRED reports Mandiant's chain: compromised X_TRADER on a 3CX employee PC -> lateral movement -> 3CX development server/build environment -> corrupted installer -> broad customer distribution. WIRED says public naming included Kimsuky, Emerald Sleet, or Velvet Chollima, but this is not the same naming as Mandiant's UNC4736. WIRED also reports CrowdStrike/SentinelOne used the name Smooth Operator and connected the operation to North Korean hackers. CSA confirms trojanized 3CX DesktopApp capable of harvesting system information and browser-stored credentials, and lists affected Windows/macOS versions."}
